TU1NZ / WANT ME SEEN — S10.2D-R4.1 HEALTH GATE START
Source-only diagnosis; no runtime or server mutation

Decision
========

The single R4 cutover failure was a deterministic health-contract
configuration error, not a startup race and not a target-process startup
failure.  The target S8 process emitted its bounded STARTING and READY states
before the S9 health unit failed.  The precise failed sub-contract was:

  S9 health unit
  -> S10.1 health envelope community check
  -> S8 Telegram health-only child process
  -> missing --runtime-release-id
  -> BOT_RUNTIME_CONTRACT_MISMATCH
  -> systemd oneshot exit 2
  -> controller collapsed the result to HEALTH_GATE_START_RED

The fixed safe controller classification is:

  HEALTH_GATE_S9_PUBLIC_HEALTH_COMMUNITY_RUNTIME_CONTRACT_RED

Evidence and classification
===========================

The privacy-safe R4 timeline was:

- 2026-09-06T12:28:35.530093Z: target S8 process STARTING.
- 2026-09-06T12:28:37.772134Z: target S8 process READY.
- 2026-09-06T12:28:53.083018Z: S9/S10 community health RED.

The process was ready about 15.3 seconds before the health failure.  Therefore
adding an arbitrary sleep would not fix this failure.  The failure is both
DETERMINISTIC_CONFIGURATION_ERROR and HEALTH_CONTRACT_ERROR.  It is not a
BOUNDED_STARTUP_TRANSITION and is not evidence of a REAL_RUNTIME_FAILURE.

Production sequence reconstructed
=================================

The existing controller sequence is preserved:

1. Verify the version-bound preflight and backup.
2. Quiesce timers, workers, landing, S8 and S10.
3. Switch Application to the frozen target commit.
4. Install target configuration and versioned Control artifacts.
5. Remove the source S8 ExecStart drop-in.
6. Install target units and health executables.
7. Run systemd daemon-reload.
8. Verify the effective PID-1 fragment, drop-ins, user, group, working
   directory and target community/release arguments.
9. Configure the already-authorized target bot profile.
10. Apply migrations 0029 and 0030.
11. Start landing, S8 and S10.
12. Wait on the existing bounded local S10 HTTP readiness loop.
13. Resume S9/S10 timers.
14. Start S8, S9 and S10 health oneshots in that order.
15. Verify systemd Result=success and ExecMainStatus=0 for every health unit.
16. Continue to target verification, rotation, the second health pass and
   final verification only if all prior gates are GREEN.

Exact root cause
================

The target PID-1 unit already supplied `--runtime-release-id s10-2d-r3-5`.
Application runtime intentionally rejects a community contract without a
release identity as BOT_RUNTIME_CONTRACT_MISMATCH.  The S8 health unit also
supplied the release identity.  However, the S9 health drop-in and S10 health
unit did not supply it to the S10.1 health envelope, and that envelope did not
forward it to the S8 health-only child process.  This made the S9 oneshot fail
deterministically after the target process was already ready.

Minimal fix
===========

- Bind both S9 and S10 health ExecStart commands to the existing frozen target
  release ID `s10-2d-r3-5`.
- Require and forward that identity in the community health child command.
- Preserve bounded child safe codes as stable process exit classes.
- Run the controller's S8/S9/S10 sequence through one privacy-safe shared
  health-gate runner.
- Emit only bounded stage, check ID, expected state, actual enum, exit class,
  systemd result, elapsed milliseconds and a deterministic SHA-256
  fingerprint.  No journal, token, DSN, Telegram payload or user data is read
  into the report.

No health expectation was weakened.  A response from the old source process
cannot satisfy target acceptance: the target S8 health command remains bound
to the expected release ID, and both community health paths now use the same
identity.

Health failure taxonomy
=======================

Only checks that actually exist in the production path are classified:

- configuration or required-file failure: exit 30
- system/service/timer state failure: exit 31
- public HTTP/health failure: exit 32
- growth/database health failure: exit 33
- Telegram channel health failure: exit 34
- community runtime/release contract failure: exit 40
- community poller failure: exit 41
- community offset failure: exit 42
- community provider failure: exit 43
- community state failure: exit 44
- community envelope failure: exit 45
- community argument failure: exit 46
- standard systemd working-directory, executable, user/group and namespace
  exits retain 200/203/216/217/226.

Unknown values remain fail-closed as PROCESS_RED.  The fingerprint excludes
elapsed time so the same contract failure has the same deterministic identity.

Source versus target health
===========================

Check                 Source expectation       Target expectation                R4 result
Application/PID       source active             target active/ready                GREEN
Local HTTP            source S10 health 200     target S10 health 200              GREEN
Public HTTP           public SFW green          public SFW green                   not the first RED
Database              source migrations absent target migrations 0029/0030        installed; not first RED
Runtime contract      source has no community   target community + release bound   RED in health child
Telegram              source bot profile        target bot profile                 preflight GREEN
Community             inactive/absent           provider and state healthy         not reached past contract
Readiness state       pre-cutover inactive      target pending, not acquisition     unchanged/fail-closed
Timers                source future runs        target future runs                 resumed before gate

Timing inventory
================

- Controller local readiness: immediate first HTTP attempt, at most 30
  attempts, one-second cadence, two-second per-request curl cap.
- S8 diagnostic child: 30-second subprocess cap; landing reads use six seconds.
- S10 health child commands: 30-second subprocess cap.
- S10 public/local reads: eight seconds per request.
- Telegram polling contract: ten-second long-poll plus its bounded transport
  allowance.
- Shared systemctl start observation: 95-second cap, allowing systemd to own
  the oneshot state transition while preventing an unbounded caller hang.
- No new retry was added to the deterministic community contract failure.

Simulator parity
================

Before R4.1 the simulator set `community_green = True` in a separate in-memory
state model.  It checked that selected strings existed in source files but did
not compute the effective S9 drop-in ExecStart, did not run the S10 community
health command builder and did not execute the controller's S8/S9/S10 health
unit sequence.  Therefore it could not observe the missing release argument.

After R4.1 the simulator imports the exact health-gate runner used by the
controller, resolves effective unit ExecStart commands including the S9
drop-in, validates the systemd-like user/group/working-directory/credential
shape, executes the real community health command builder, and preserves the
same exit-to-safe-code mapping.  The pre-fix missing-binding fixture is
expected RED; the fixed target path is GREEN.

Nine health cases cover normal target start, absent target process, wrong
release, HTTP failure, database failure, the existing bounded startup
transition, a still-answering source with no target, missing community
contract, and the exact R4 missing health release binding.  The full release
suite separately covers target success, health-start rollback, bot failure,
rotation failure, community failure, rollback, active-acquisition protection
and migration/evidence edge cases.

Boundaries
==========

Application source, migrations, bot cursor/lease contract and public runtime
are unchanged.  Adult media, AVS, payment, publishing, controlled beta,
production and real acquisition remain closed.  No server command, deployment,
BotFather operation or production migration is part of R4.1.
